Fibromyalgia
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain, Multiple specific tender points, Unrefreshing sleep/severe fatigue, Normal inflammatory markers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-013
Reason for Visit: Severe, incapacitating total-body pain flare and extreme exhaustion leading to an inability to cope at home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a severe acute-on-chronic pain crisis with profound generalized fatigue and resulting functional collapse. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out an acute inflammatory polyarthritis flare, occult systemic infection, or severe neurological deficit, and to immediately break the pain/insomnia cycle to prevent impending hospital presentation before daytime services open.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every single muscle in my body feels like it's been beaten with a hammer, I haven't slept in three days, and I can't think straight."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 3-day severe exacerbation of chronic, widespread musculoskeletal pain, present for over 2 years. The pain is described as a severe, deep ache accompanied by intense allodynia (pain provoked by clothing rubbing against skin). Patient reports completely unrefreshing, severely fragmented sleep, leading to profound, unremitting generalized fatigue and significantly decreased physical stamina. Subjective cognitive difficulties ('fibro fog') are prominent tonight. Symptoms spiked following a recent severe tension headache and high situational stress.
Functional Status: Currently unable to complete ADLs due to severe pain and exhaustion; mobilizing with extreme difficulty.
Social/Safety Concerns: High degree of acute distress and carer fatigue; patient is expressing feelings of complete helplessness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Moving very stiffly, tearful, frequently changing position to find comfort but failing.
Physical Exam: Exquisite, severe tenderness upon light palpation of multiple specific predefined anatomic tender points across the body (occiput, trapezius, supraspinatus, gluteal, greater trochanter). Complete absence of hot, red, or swollen joints. Muscle tone is normal without focal weakness; deep tendon reflexes are intact and symmetrical. Documented history shows entirely normal inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), normal creatine kinase, and negative specific autoantibodies.
Environmental Observations: House is quiet; patient is using a heavy weighted blanket which appears to be exacerbating the allodynia.
Medication Reconciliation: Overusing paracetamol and codeine today with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fibromyalgia - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out due to complete absence of clinical joint swelling, warmth, or synovitis, and historically negative autoimmune markers.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out as pain is widespread rather than strictly proximal (shoulder/pelvic girdle), and historically normal ESR/CRP.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Unlikely due to absence of malar rash, photosensitivity, mucosal ulcers, or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe pain crisis and functional collapse that rendered them unable to manage basic ADLs at home. The severity of the acute-on-chronic pain mandated an immediate clinical examination to definitively rule out acute systemic inflammatory conditions, acute myopathy, or joint sepsis. Clinically confirming the absence of acute structural or inflammatory disease allowed for immediate, targeted pharmacological intervention (neuromodulation rather than standard analgesia) to break the escalating cycle of pain and insomnia. This timely intervention successfully avoided a highly inappropriate emergency department admission, satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of low-dose Amitriptyline (10mg nocte) to restore sleep architecture and provide central neuromodulation. Strongly advised immediate cessation of ineffective opiate analgesics.
Positive Behavioral Change: Replace heavy weighted blankets and tight clothing with loose, soft fabrics, and engage in 5 minutes of slow, warm-water showering. The gentle, diffuse sensory input of warm water will safely override the hyperactive pain pathways (gate control), rewarding the patient with an immediate reduction in allodynia and muscle tension.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a multidisciplinary pain management clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad for targeted comfort.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to monitor the efficacy of the sleep intervention.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever, sudden hot and swollen joints, sudden true muscle weakness (inability to lift limbs), or acute loss of bowel/bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fibromyalgia is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion; should new localized swelling or fevers occur, the clinical picture must be re-evaluated.
Clinical Course: This condition is characterized by a central nervous system that is 'turned up too loud', amplifying normal sensory signals into severe pain. Flares are exhausting but do not cause permanent joint or muscle damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if any specific joint becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you develop new, true weakness in your arms or legs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Traditional painkillers like codeine do not work well for fibromyalgia because the issue is the brain's pain processing, not tissue damage. Amitriptyline acts directly on the central nervous system to turn down the 'volume' of the pain signals and restore restorative sleep.
Actionable Steps: Take the Amitriptyline strictly 2 hours before bed. Prioritizing deep sleep will directly lower your nervous system's reactivity tomorrow, reinforcing the critical importance of sleep hygiene over daytime painkillers.

